<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Plauen</h1><p class="st-meta">1209 CE – 1572 CE (approx.) · capital Plauen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Vogtland lordship of the imperial advocates of Plauen, whose family gave the Vogtland its name and a Grand Master to the Teutonic Order.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The advocates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weida">Weida</a>, imperial officials whose men were all named Henry, governed lands on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">Elster</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors. From about 1209 a branch ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a>, and the whole region came to be called the Vogtland after their title. Over time they treated the office as a hereditary lordship.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords ruled a hilly district of farms, forests and small towns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a>. Family members served as knights and administrators, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Marienburg</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> and became Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins</a> and Bohemian kings steadily eroded their power; in 1466 the Wettins took Plauen.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-plauen">Henry IV of Plauen</a>, Burgrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and Bohemian chancellor, recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a> in 1547. His line died out in 1572 and the lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Another branch of the family survived as the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">Reuss</a>, rulers in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> until 1918.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1209 CE – 1572 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.76%;width:7.22%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1209 CE – 1572 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plauen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Heinrich von Plauen, Henry IV of Plauen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vogtland, Advocates of Weida</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1390&amp;focus=lordship-of-plauen" aria-label="Lordship of Plauen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4957 424 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Plauen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5277 680L5229 694M5277 680L5330 692M5277 680L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5330 692h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5277 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5277 680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Plauen. <a href="/atlas?year=1390&amp;focus=lordship-of-plauen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
